External JavaScript code is not visible in Kotlin by default. To use JavaScript code in Kotlin, you can describe its API with external declarations.","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations"],"line_start":13,"line_end":15,"score":0.786994457244873,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"external-interfaces","start":2008,"end":2612,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"6ef84fc36bcda64a15353bd9bda36bf7eae59bdef82b47d47a7e663a56cffb33","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"External interfaces

You can describe the shape of a JavaScript object in Kotlin. Consider this JavaScript function and what it returns:

function createUser (name, age) {
    return { name: name, age: age };
}

See how its shape can be described in Kotlin with an external interface User type:

external interface User : JsAny {
    val name: String
    val age: Int
}

external fun createUser(name: String, age: Int): User

External interfaces don't have runtime type information and are a compile-time-only concept. Therefore, external interfaces have some restrictions compared to regular interfaces:","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations > External interfaces"],"line_start":72,"line_end":89,"score":0.7023055553436279,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"javascript-classes","start":1509,"end":2006,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"63c503af6e7df2ddfa0a0c18f1e2c3dd6f32f8f10c4bee3e6df5dcf0ecce0c11","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"JavaScript classes

Consider this JavaScript class:

class Rectangle {
    constructor (height, width) {
        this.height = height;
        this.width = width;
    }

    area () {
        return this.height * this.width;
    }
}

You can use it in Kotlin as an external class:

external class Rectangle(height: Double, width: Double) : JsAny {
    val height: Double
    val width: Double
    fun area(): Double
}

All declarations inside the external class are implicitly considered external.","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations > JavaScript classes"],"line_start":47,"line_end":70,"score":0.7016766667366028,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"external-interfaces","start":4581,"end":5550,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"58093ca4b4628d8399f47d21b58ffcc69ea509a005f083d2b58af6b0227e6c35","document_id":"js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"external interfaces

JavaScript does not have the concept of interfaces. When a function expects its parameter to support two methods foo and bar , you would just pass in an object that actually has these methods.

You can use interfaces to express this concept in statically typed Kotlin:

external interface HasFooAndBar {
    fun foo()

    fun bar()
}

external fun myFunction(p: HasFooAndBar)

A typical use case for external interfaces is to describe settings objects. For example:

external interface JQueryAjaxSettings {
    var async: Boolean

    var cache: Boolean

    var complete: (JQueryXHR, String) -> Unit

    // etc
}

fun JQueryAjaxSettings(): JQueryAjaxSettings = js("{}")

external class JQuery {
    companion object {
        fun get(settings: JQueryAjaxSettings): JQueryXHR
    }
}

fun sendQuery() {
    JQuery.get(JQueryAjaxSettings().apply {
        complete = { (xhr, data) ->
            window.alert("Request complete")
        }
    })
}","title":"Use JavaScript code from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-interop.html","sections":["Use JavaScript code from Kotlin > external modifier > external interfaces"],"line_start":126,"line_end":166,"score":0.7011271715164185,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"jsexport-annotation","start":3701,"end":4560,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"3e36da4b83ae9f33e669108aeee6a8671d7327481edcc5e401cf3f55d2ed1476","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"@JsExport annotation

By applying the @JsExport annotation to a top-level declaration (like a class, interface, or function), you can make Kotlin declarations available from JavaScript or TypeScript. The annotation exports all nested declarations with the name given in Kotlin.

For example, here's how you can export a Kotlin interface with a nested class and a named companion object:

@JsExport
interface Identity {
     class Metadata(val tag: String)

    companion object Registry {
        val defaultTag = "GUEST"
    }
}

Currently, the @JsExport annotation is the only way to make your functions visible from Kotlin.

The @JsExport annotation is also available:

In common code in multiplatform projects. It only has an effect when compiling for the JavaScript target and allows you to also export Kotlin declarations that are not platform-specific.","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsExport annotation"],"line_start":79,"line_end":98,"score":0.6990640163421631,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

{"question":"How do I declare an existing JavaScript API so Kotlin can call it without providing an implementation?","candidates":[{"anchor":"external-modifier","start":1592,"end":2598,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"ea1bb45f19d675790de3284d7eebb628497bbd4954c59494aa2d1330f9aadb36","document_id":"js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"external modifier

To tell Kotlin that a certain declaration is written in pure JavaScript, you should mark it with the external modifier. When the compiler sees such a declaration, it assumes that the implementation for the corresponding class, function or property is provided externally (by the developer or via an npm dependency ), and therefore does not try to generate any JavaScript code from the declaration. This is also why external declarations can't have a body. For example:

external fun alert(message: Any?): Unit

external class Node {
    val firstChild: Node

    fun append(child: Node): Node

    fun removeChild(child: Node): Node

    // etc
}

external val window: Window

Note that the external modifier is inherited by nested declarations. This is why in the example Node class, there is no external modifier before member functions and properties.

The external modifier is only allowed on package-level declarations. You can't declare an external member of a non- external class.","title":"Use JavaScript code from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-interop.html","sections":["Use JavaScript code from Kotlin > external modifier"],"line_start":47,"line_end":67,"score":0.6989555358886719,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"javascript-functions","start":870,"end":1207,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"698ddd9815a5ad680a01cc1085579ec13daef62762c334316265cf4342c89d6a","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"JavaScript functions

Consider this JavaScript function:

function greet (name) {
    console.log("Hello, " + name + "!");
}

You can declare it in Kotlin as an external function:

external fun greet(name: String)

External functions don't have bodies, and you can call it as a regular Kotlin function:

fun main() {
    greet("Alice")
}","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations > JavaScript functions"],"line_start":17,"line_end":33,"score":0.6929423809051514,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"external-objects","start":2849,"end":3171,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"c83c321a634a492bb9aee13043d4dab4b0eca0d1ab84664e26dd245c0cd0cbc5","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"External objects

Consider these JavaScript variables holding an object:

let Counter = {
    value: 0,
    step: 1,
    increment () {
        this.value += this.step;
    }
};

You can use them in Kotlin as an external object:

external object Counter : JsAny {
    fun increment()
    val value: Int
    var step: Int
}","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations > External objects"],"line_start":99,"line_end":117,"score":0.685716986656189,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"callable-javascript-objects-with-nativeinvoke","start":3438,"end":4170,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"f67b3934e9e2471adc4d4cfffe63b41d79ff03d95ba5ce43ee057b84d75fff65","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Callable JavaScript objects with @nativeInvoke

You can use the @nativeInvoke annotation on a Kotlin member function of an external declaration (a class or an interface) to make it callable as a JavaScript function.

With this annotation, each call to that function in Kotlin translates to a direct call to the JavaScript object:

import kotlin.js.nativeInvoke

@OptIn(ExperimentalWasmJsInterop::class)
external class JsAction {
    @nativeInvoke
    operator fun invoke(data: String)
}

fun main() {
    val action = JsAction() 
    action("Run task")
}

The @nativeInvoke annotation is a temporary solution until there's a design for stable interoperability. Currently, when you use @nativeInvoke , the compiler reports a warning.","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > External declarations > Callable JavaScript objects with @nativeInvoke"],"line_start":123,"line_end":142,"score":0.6796059012413025,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"javascript-modules","start":5580,"end":6219,"split_code":false,"platforms":["wasm"],"id":"e4ec9b6e192fd837189d5498463708d3d0c8752c0ee08a5dbfa81944783d1cea","document_id":"wasm-js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"JavaScript modules

By default, external declarations correspond to the JavaScript global scope. If you annotate a Kotlin file with the @JsModule annotation , then all external declarations within it are imported from the specified module.

Consider this JavaScript code sample:

// users.mjs
export let maxUsers = 10;

export class User {
    constructor (username) {
        this.username = username;
    }
}

Use this JavaScript code in Kotlin with the @JsModule annotation:

// Kotlin
@file:JsModule("./users.mjs")

external val maxUsers: Int

external class User : JsAny {
    constructor(username: String)

    val username: String
}","title":"Kotlin/Wasm JavaScript interoperability","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/wasm-js-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with JavaScript > Use JavaScript code in Kotlin > JavaScript modules"],"line_start":180,"line_end":206,"score":0.6790966391563416,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

