Previously, Kotlin variance information in generic positions was lost when exporting types to TypeScript.

With Kotlin 2.4.0, variance annotation is now saved during export and mapped to TypeScript's variance annotations .

In your Kotlin code, define the variance of your generic type parameters:

// Kotlin
// 'out' signals covariance (the interface only produces T)
interface Producer<out T> {
    fun produce(): T
}

// 'in' signals contravariance (the interface only consumes T)
interface Consumer<in T> {
    fun consume(item: T)
}

With Kotlin 2.4.0, the in and out keywords are preserved in the generated TypeScript output:

// Generated .d.ts
export interface Producer<out T> {
    produce(): T;
}

export interface Consumer<in T> {
    consume(item: T): void;
}","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.4.0","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew24.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.4.0 > Kotlin/JS > Preserve type variance when exporting to TypeScript"],"line_start":747,"line_end":775,"score":0.6418926119804382,"release_version":"2.4.0","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."},{"anchor":"kotlin-native","start":19416,"end":19627,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"8d7c45fc4ce120a7e7ccace9e4c7b4d0ad1046ed724b36798fc2e1b867e2aa86","document_id":"whatsnew24","strategy":"semantic","text":"Kotlin/Native

