All Kotlin exceptions are unchecked, meaning that errors are caught at runtime. However, Swift has only checked errors that are handled at compile time. So, if Swift or Objective-C code calls a Kotlin method that throws an exception, the Kotlin method should be marked with the @Throws annotation, specifying a list of "expected" exception classes.

When compiling to the Swift/Objective-C framework, non- suspend functions that have or inherit the @Throws annotation are represented as NSError* -producing methods in Objective-C and as throws methods in Swift. Representations for suspend functions always have an NSError* / Error parameter in the completion handler.

When a Kotlin function called from Swift/Objective-C code throws an exception which is an instance of one of the classes specified with @Throws or their subclasses, the exception is propagated as an NSError . Other Kotlin exceptions reaching Swift/Objective-C are considered unhandled and cause program termination.

suspend functions without @Throws propagate only CancellationException (as NSError ). Non- suspend functions without @Throws don't propagate Kotlin exceptions at all.

Note that the opposite reversed translation is not implemented yet: Swift/Objective-C error-throwing methods aren't imported to Kotlin as exception-throwing.","title":"Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/native-objc-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C > Mappings > Errors and exceptions"],"line_start":218,"line_end":228,"score":0.771030068397522,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"errors-and-exceptions","start":11591,"end":11639,"split_code":false,"platforms":["native"],"id":"b45dd59fd11db243dbb6247462f3555de577c5a1185b05af9bf9a2e22a01f91a","document_id":"native-objc-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"See an example in the Kotlin-Swift interopedia .","title":"Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/native-objc-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C > Mappings > Errors and exceptions"],"line_start":230,"line_end":230,"score":0.657356858253479,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"checked-exceptions","start":14615,"end":15379,"split_code":false,"platforms":["jvm","android"],"id":"3383ca6be2ad1ad89624ab8216be907729ec65679fcf44910c153eb274d20433","document_id":"java-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Checked exceptions

Kotlin does not have checked exceptions. So, normally the Java signatures of Kotlin functions do not declare exceptions thrown. Thus, if you have a function in Kotlin like this:

// example.kt
package demo

fun writeToFile() {
    /*...*/
    throw IOException()
}

And you want to call it from Java and catch the exception:

// Java
try {
    demo.Example.writeToFile();
} catch (IOException e) { 
    // error: writeToFile() does not declare IOException in the throws list
    // ...
}

You get an error message from the Java compiler, because writeToFile() does not declare IOException . To work around this problem, use the @Throws annotation in Kotlin:

@Throws(IOException::class)
fun writeToFile() {
    /*...*/
    throw IOException()
}","title":"Calling Kotlin from Java","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/java-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Calling Kotlin from Java > Checked exceptions"],"line_start":409,"line_end":437,"score":0.6564631462097168,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"checked-exceptions","start":27144,"end":27545,"split_code":false,"platforms":["jvm","android"],"id":"38a9d8bdd89541dba8fb80f972d3426f4b3f5645967183d7ce98d698170aa968","document_id":"java-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Checked exceptions

In Kotlin, all exceptions are unchecked , meaning that the compiler does not force you to catch any of them. So, when you call a Java method that declares a checked exception, Kotlin does not force you to do anything:

fun render(list: List<*>, to: Appendable) {
    for (item in list) {
        to.append(item.toString()) // Java would require us to catch IOException here
    }
}","title":"Calling Java from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/java-interop.html","sections":["Calling Java from Kotlin > Checked exceptions"],"line_start":585,"line_end":593,"score":0.6436998844146729,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"extensions-and-category-members","start":13029,"end":13898,"split_code":false,"platforms":["native"],"id":"0f9e13a5cf84ac7f942d4fceb26d486bc876be94d3151364679c05ae89009d03","document_id":"native-objc-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Extensions and category members

Members of Objective-C categories and Swift extensions are generally imported to Kotlin as extensions. That's why these declarations can't be overridden in Kotlin, and the extension initializers aren't available as Kotlin constructors.

Currently, there are two exceptions. Starting with Kotlin 1.8.20, category members that are declared in the same headers as the NSView class (from the AppKit framework) or UIView classes (from the UIKit framework) are imported as members of these classes. This means that you can override methods that subclass from NSView or UIView.

Kotlin extensions to "regular" Kotlin classes are imported to Swift and Objective-C as extensions and category members, respectively. Kotlin extensions to other types are treated as top-level declarations with an additional receiver parameter. These types include:","title":"Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/native-objc-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C > Mappings > Extensions and category members"],"line_start":269,"line_end":275,"score":0.6306930780410767,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

